.TH "OSTRYA\-SEND" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-send \- Serve one pull session over standard input and standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya send [OPTIONS] [REFSPECS]...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Serve one pull session over standard input and standard output.
.PP
Without \%\-\-one\-way, an ssh pull runs this command on the remote side. The command answers each \fB\%Get\fR path as the archive view of \fB\%ostrya serve\fR answers a \fB\%GET\fR of that path. It takes no lock and writes no file in the repository.
.PP
The client name is \%\-\-client, else the value of \fB\%LOGNAME\fR, which sshd sets from the password entry of the user. With neither, and with a name that the item \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR refuses, the command writes an error line and exits \%1 before it opens the repository. At start, the command reads and checks the client groups and the server group of the repository config. It reads no key group. A bad group ends it with status \%1.
.PP
The session serves the refs that the client group of the item \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR reads, as \fB\%ostrya serve\fR serves them to that item. When the repository has client groups and none carries the item, the command writes an error line and exits \%1 before it reads standard input. The session uses no \fB\%anonymous\fR and no \fB\%net:\fR item. In a repository with no client group, the session takes the item \fB\%anonymous\fR, so it serves each local ref and each mirror ref. \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR give the summary of the item, as \fB\%ostrya serve\fR gives it.
.PP
Standard output carries the frames of the session alone. On failure, the command writes \fB\%error: MESSAGE\fR to standard error. When a ref listing finds a directory under \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR whose name is not a collection id, the command writes one \fB\%warning:\fR line that names it to standard error, one time in each session. When the summary of the item does not match the client groups, the session answers \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR as absent paths, and the command writes one \fB\%error:\fR line to standard error, one time in each session. It exits \%0 when standard input ends at a frame boundary, and \%1 after an \fB\%Error\fR frame or a failed write.
.PP
An \fB\%authorized_keys\fR entry with \fB\%command="ostrya send \-\-repo=PATH"\fR limits an ssh key to a pull from one repository.
.PP
With \%\-\-one\-way, the command serves no session. It writes the commits of the refspecs to standard output as one \%one\-way stream, and it does not read standard input. It reads no client group and no \fB\%LOGNAME\fR\&. It holds the repository lock as shared while it writes the stream. Standard output carries the stream alone. When the command refuses the options, it writes the usage error of \fB\%send\fR to standard error and exits \%1. A failure after the command reads the options writes one \fB\%error: MESSAGE\fR line to standard error and exits \%1. On success, the command exits \%0 and writes nothing to standard error, also under \%\-\-verbose. A build without the feature \fB\%push\fR has no \%\-\-one\-way.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIREFSPECS...\fR
The refspecs of \%\-\-one\-way, each \fB\%SRC[:DST]\fR\&. At least one refspec is required.
.IP
SRC is a revision of this repository, and DST is the ref that the receiver sets to its commit: a local ref NAME, or \%@ID:NAME, the ref NAME of the collection ID. The command splits each refspec with the rule of \fB\%push\fR: DST is the last ref of the refspec, so \fB\%main:@X:a\fR sends \fB\%main\fR to \fB\%@X:a\fR\&. If SRC is a ref, DST defaults to the text of SRC. A SRC that is a checksum, or that has a \fB\%^\fR suffix, needs a DST. A \%one\-way stream deletes no ref, so the command refuses \fB\%:DST\fR\&.
.IP
The command refuses a DST that is not NAME or \%@ID:NAME under the \%ref\-name rule, which includes a DST with \fB\%^\fR\&. It also refuses a plain DST of \%64 lowercase hex characters. Without \%\-\-remote, the reason is that a revision reads such a name as a commit checksum. With \%\-\-remote, the command refuses it by the refspec rule of \fB\%push\fR, and it refuses a DST \%@ID:NAME, because a remote ref has no collection.
.IP
If the commit of a SRC has a ref binding, the binding must hold the name part of DST, unless \%\-\-disable\-verify\-bindings is given. For a DST \%@ID:NAME, the commit must be bound to the collection ID, also with \%\-\-disable\-verify\-bindings. Otherwise the command writes \fB\%error: binding\-mismatch: MESSAGE\fR before it writes a byte, and exits \%1. \fB\%ostrya commit \-b BRANCH\fR binds the commit to BRANCH. The refspecs need \%\-\-one\-way.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-client\fR \fINAME\fR
The client name of the session, in place of \fB\%LOGNAME\fR\&.
.IP
The session serves the refs of the client group that carries the item \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR\&. NAME is not empty, holds no control character, and does not start or end with white space. The option is safe only under a forced \fB\%command=\fR in \fB\%authorized_keys\fR, because a user with a shell can give any name.
.TP
\fB\-\-one\-way\fR
Write the commits of the refspecs to standard output as one \%one\-way stream.
.IP
The stream carries each commit, the objects of its tree, and its detached metadata. It also carries each dependency that field \%2 of a commit names, with no ref update. It ends with a \fB\%Commit\fR frame that holds one ref update for each refspec. The stream carries no parent commit. The command does no negotiation, so it sends each object.
.IP
Each update expects any state of the ref, so the receiver applies its own rules. A failure before the first byte writes nothing to standard output. A failure after the first byte ends the stream with \fB\%Abort\fR, and a receiver changes nothing. The option conflicts with \%\-\-client.
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fIREMOTE\fR
Prefix each DST with REMOTE, so the receiver writes the remote ref \%REMOTE:DST.
.IP
REMOTE is a remote name: one or more alphanumeric characters, \fB\%\-\fR, \fB\%_\fR, and \fB\%\&.\fR, that starts with an alphanumeric character or \fB\%_\fR\&. The option needs \%\-\-one\-way.
.TP
\fB\-\-compress\fR[=\fILEVEL\fR]
Send the content objects deflated at LEVEL, \%1 to \%9.
.IP
The value needs the \fB\%=\fR form, and \%\-\-compress alone gives level \%6. Without this option, the stream carries the content objects raw. With it, if this repository is an archive repository, the stream carries each stored content object as it is, whatever LEVEL is. The object keeps the zlib level that the repository wrote it with. The option needs \%\-\-one\-way.
.TP
\fB\-\-disable\-verify\-bindings\fR
Do not check the \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR list of the commit of each DST.
.IP
A receiver whose rules check the list refuses such a stream with \fB\%binding\-mismatch\fR, so the receiver also needs \%\-\-disable\-verify\-bindings. The switch skips no check of \fB\%ostree.collection\-binding\fR\&. The option needs \%\-\-one\-way.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
